{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792530951659,"sessionID":"ses_ea4d52decffeaHsc6A3RWeHtGB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_AlCYse0rp0qHs5KJV5sW2568","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-20\\logs\\critique.prompt.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-20\\logs\\critique.prompt.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # one-a-day — 2026-10-20 — phase: critique\n2: \n3: You are the coding agent running one phase of today's **one-a-day** run, in the\n4: repo at `C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day`. Nobody is watching. Nobody can answer a question. You have\n5: one job this call, stated under \"Your task\" below, and you finish it by writing\n6: files. Then you stop.\n7: \n8: ## Files are your only memory\n9: \n10: Every earlier phase today left its result in `journal/2026-10-20/`. Read what is\n11: there before you do anything:\n12: \n13: - `journal/2026-10-20/state.json` — the phase ledger. A phase is done when its key is\n14:   `\"done\"`. The critique phase also writes `decision` (`ship` | `no-ship`) and\n15:   `pick` (the variant letter).\n16: - `journal/2026-10-20/run.json` — who is running (the runner fills `runner.*`), and\n17:   `model.selfDeclared`, which YOU fill (below).\n18: - `journal/2026-10-20/10-scout.md`, `20-brief.md`, `30-build.md`, `40-critique.md`,\n19:   `50-ship.md` — the day's story, one file per phase.\n20: - `journal/2026-10-20/variants/<a|b|c>/` — each built variant: `src/` (its code),\n21:   `renders/`, `stills/`, `report.json`.\n22: \n23: When your task is complete, set `state.json.critique = \"done\"` (merge, do not\n24: overwrite other keys) as the LAST thing you do. Never set it early: the runner\n25: re-calls this phase until it sees that key, and a half-written output marked\n26: done ships a half-written day.\n27: \n28: ## Declare yourself\n29: \n30: The runner asked for `opencode`. In your FIRST action this phase, write into\n31: `journal/2026-10-20/run.json` the key `model.selfDeclared` with the model you believe\n32: you are (e.g. `claude-fable-5.1`, `gpt-5-codex`, `kimi-k2`), merged into the\n33: existing JSON (keep every other key). If you cannot tell, write `\"unknown\"`. A\n34: human may later set `model.corrected`; never touch that key.\n35: \n36: ## Read before you write\n37: \n38: `AGENTS.md` at the repo root is the contract for this repo: the loop, the\n39: checklist, the rules that fail silently. The reasoning behind it is in\n40: `node_modules/@m0saic/knowledge/README.md` (start there, then `docs/m0saic-thesis.md`,\n41: then `docs/README.md`, the router). For template work the pages that matter most are\n42: `docs/templates/philosophy-and-contract.md`, `docs/templates/construction-strategy.md`,\n43: `docs/templates/standalone-pack-authoring.md`,\n44: `docs/handbook/feasibility-precision-quantization.md` and `docs/runtime/cli-usage.md`.\n45: \n46: ## Hard rules (the runner enforces every one of them after you; break one and the day is discarded)\n47: \n48: - Never `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git reset`, `git tag`, `npm publish`.\n49:   The runner commits exactly once at the end of the day and pushes.\n50: - Never edit `pipeline/`, `tools/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `.github/`,\n51:   `package.json`, `dep-allowlist.json`, `frozen.manifest.json`, or any other day's\n52:   `journal/` folder. Never run `check-freeze --update`.\n53: - Never modify a template folder that already exists at HEAD (`src/<pack>/<slug>/vN/`\n54:   that is not today's). Shipped templates are frozen; a fix is a new `vN+1`.\n55: - Today produces AT MOST ONE new template folder under `src/`. Variants share its\n56:   id and folder; the last variant left in place is what the critic judges last.\n57: - No new dependencies. No downloaded media into the repo. No secrets, no wall-clock\n58:   reads, no `Math.random` in template code.\n59: - Write scratch only under `journal/2026-10-20/` (renders, notes, experiments).\n60: - If something is broken that you cannot fix inside this phase's scope, write what\n61:   you found into this phase's output file and stop WITHOUT marking the phase done.\n62:   A day that ships nothing is fine. A day that ships something wrong is not.\n63: \n64: ## Writing for the journal\n65: \n66: The journal is public and is read by people and by tomorrow's agent. Short\n67: sections, real links, numbers only when they change a decision, honest about\n68: what is weak. Markdown, ASCII quotes, no marketing voice.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: # Your task\n73: \n74: \n75: Judge today's variants. You are the critic: you do NOT edit code. Read\n76: `journal/2026-10-20/20-brief.md` (the rubric), `30-build.md`, and every\n77: `journal/2026-10-20/variants/<x>/report.json`. Look at the stills in\n78: `variants/<x>/stills/` if you can view images. If you cannot, judge from\n79: `report.json` (exit codes, degraded flags, probe dims), the variant's\n80: `src/<slug>.ts` and `src/<slug>.layout.m0` (the flattened layout — read the\n81: rectangle counts and proportions), and `node tools/check-registry.mjs --json`.\n82: \n83: Be adversarial. The default is \"no ship\". A template earns a ship.\n84: \n85: Read every still the way a stranger would, string by string, before you score\n86: anything. A number that is malformed (`2:030.123` for a lap time), a sign\n87: that is missing, a unit nothing explains, defaults that contradict what the\n88: brief's \"Defaults must show\" promised: each is a defect on line 2 or line 8,\n89: and none of them is in `report.json`. Day 013's first critique found the\n90: missing bars and passed the malformed times beside them.\n91: \n92: A no-ship is not the end of the day: the build phase is called again with\n93: your verdict in hand, and you (a fresh call) judge the result. So \"what each\n94: variant gets wrong\" is the builder's worklist — name every defect you found,\n95: specifically, not only the worst one.\n96: \n97: ## Score each variant, 0–2 on each line\n98: \n99: 1. Renders at defaults, every canvas, no error mosaic (exit 0 everywhere; a 3 anywhere is fatal)\n100: 2. The defaults SHOW the use case — a stranger sees what it is for\n101: 3. Text fits and reads at portrait and square, not only landscape\n102: 4. Layout is honest rectangles: proportions hold across aspects, nothing overlaps or collapses; the\n103:    layout contract promises what the brief said (read `layoutContract()` in `src/<slug>.ts`: text fits,\n104:    the bands the design depends on - not a ruler widened until it passes)\n105: 5. Props are the few that matter, typed, with sane defaults; a user could actually feed it\n106: 6. Would a person in the scouted community use this instead of what they do now?\n107: 7. Code quality against AGENTS.md: deterministic, `ctx.target`, bound text, fitted copy, ASCII\n108: 8. The brief was honest — the variant does what `20-brief.md` promised\n109: 9. The why-tutorial tells the truth — `stills/tutorial-2.png` (the problem)\n110:    cites what `10-scout.md` found, in the evidence's words, with its sources;\n111:    `stills/tutorial-3.png` (the solution) describes what THIS variant does,\n112:    not what the brief hoped; `tutorial-5.png` is the template at its defaults;\n113:    `tutorial-6.png` (how it was made) shows the phases that actually ran\n114: \n115: Fatal regardless of score: a degraded render, a test that only passes because\n116: it asserts nothing, a prop with no default, a pack or slug that misrepresents\n117: what it is, a why-tutorial whose problem page names sources the scout never\n118: opened or whose solution page claims a feature the variant does not have.\n119: \n120: ## Output — `journal/2026-10-20/40-critique.md`\n121: \n122: ```\n123: # Critique — 2026-10-20\n124: \n125: ## Scores\n126: | variant | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | total | fatal? |\n127: ## What each variant gets right / wrong (three lines each, specific: which still, which prop)\n128: ## Decision: SHIP <letter> | NO SHIP\n129: ## If ship: what a human polish pass should look at first\n130: ## If no ship: the one thing that would have changed the verdict\n131: ```\n132: \n133: Then set `state.json.decision` to `\"ship\"` or `\"no-ship\"`, `state.json.pick` to\n134: the letter (or null), `noShipReason` when no-ship (one line), and finally\n135: `critique: \"done\"`.\n\n(End of file - total 135 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# one-a-day — 2026-10-20 — phase: critique\n\nYou are the coding agent running one phase of today's **one-a-day** run, in the\nrepo at `C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day`. Nobody is watching. Nobody can answer a question. You have\none job this call, stated under \"Your task\" below, and you finish it by writing\nfiles. Then you stop.\n\n## Files are your only memory\n\nEvery earlier phase today left its result in `journal/2026-10-20/`. Read what is\nthere before you do anything:\n\n- `journal/2026-10-20/state.json` — the phase ledger. A phase is done when its key is\n  `\"done\"`. The critique phase also writes `decision` (`ship` | `no-ship`) and\n  `pick` (the variant letter).\n- `journal/2026-10-20/run.json` — who is running (the runner fills `runner.*`), and\n  `model.selfDeclared`, which YOU fill (below).\n- `journal/2026-10-20/10-scout.md`, `20-brief.md`, `30-build.md`, `40-critique.md`,\n  `50-ship.md` — the day's story, one file per phase.\n- `journal/2026-10-20/variants/<a|b|c>/` — each built variant: `src/` (its code),","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-20\\logs\\critique.prompt.md","text":"# one-a-day — 2026-10-20 — phase: critique\n\nYou are the coding agent running one phase of today's **one-a-day** run, in the\nrepo at `C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day`. Nobody is watching. Nobody can answer a question. You have\none job this call, stated under \"Your task\" below, and you finish it by writing\nfiles. Then you stop.\n\n## Files are your only memory\n\nEvery earlier phase today left its result in `journal/2026-10-20/`. Read what is\nthere before you do anything:\n\n- `journal/2026-10-20/state.json` — the phase ledger. A phase is done when its key is\n  `\"done\"`. The critique phase also writes `decision` (`ship` | `no-ship`) and\n  `pick` (the variant letter).\n- `journal/2026-10-20/run.json` — who is running (the runner fills `runner.*`), and\n  `model.selfDeclared`, which YOU fill (below).\n- `journal/2026-10-20/10-scout.md`, `20-brief.md`, `30-build.md`, `40-critique.md`,\n  `50-ship.md` — the day's story, one file per phase.\n- `journal/2026-10-20/variants/<a|b|c>/` — each built variant: `src/` (its code),\n  `renders/`, `stills/`, `report.json`.\n\nWhen your task is complete, set `state.json.critique = \"done\"` (merge, do not\noverwrite other keys) as the LAST thing you do. Never set it early: the runner\nre-calls this phase until it sees that key, and a half-written output marked\ndone ships a half-written day.\n\n## Declare yourself\n\nThe runner asked for `opencode`. In your FIRST action this phase, write into\n`journal/2026-10-20/run.json` the key `model.selfDeclared` with the model you believe\nyou are (e.g. `claude-fable-5.1`, `gpt-5-codex`, `kimi-k2`), merged into the\nexisting JSON (keep every other key). If you cannot tell, write `\"unknown\"`. A\nhuman may later set `model.corrected`; never touch that key.\n\n## Read before you write\n\n`AGENTS.md` at the repo root is the contract for this repo: the loop, the\nchecklist, the rules that fail silently. The reasoning behind it is in\n`node_modules/@m0saic/knowledge/README.md` (start there, then `docs/m0saic-thesis.md`,\nthen `docs/README.md`, the router). For template work the pages that matter most are\n`docs/templates/philosophy-and-contract.md`, `docs/templates/construction-strategy.md`,\n`docs/templates/standalone-pack-authoring.md`,\n`docs/handbook/feasibility-precision-quantization.md` and `docs/runtime/cli-usage.md`.\n\n## Hard rules (the runner enforces every one of them after you; break one and the day is discarded)\n\n- Never `git commit`, `git push`, `git checkout`, `git reset`, `git tag`, `npm publish`.\n  The runner commits exactly once at the end of the day and pushes.\n- Never edit `pipeline/`, `tools/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `.github/`,\n  `package.json`, `dep-allowlist.json`, `frozen.manifest.json`, or any other day's\n  `journal/` folder. Never run `check-freeze --update`.\n- Never modify a template folder that already exists at HEAD (`src/<pack>/<slug>/vN/`\n  that is not today's). Shipped templates are frozen; a fix is a new `vN+1`.\n- Today produces AT MOST ONE new template folder under `src/`. Variants share its\n  id and folder; the last variant left in place is what the critic judges last.\n- No new dependencies. No downloaded media into the repo. No secrets, no wall-clock\n  reads, no `Math.random` in template code.\n- Write scratch only under `journal/2026-10-20/` (renders, notes, experiments).\n- If something is broken that you cannot fix inside this phase's scope, write what\n  you found into this phase's output file and stop WITHOUT marking the phase done.\n  A day that ships nothing is fine. A day that ships something wrong is not.\n\n## Writing for the journal\n\nThe journal is public and is read by people and by tomorrow's agent. Short\nsections, real links, numbers only when they change a decision, honest about\nwhat is weak. Markdown, ASCII quotes, no marketing voice.\n\n---\n\n# Your task\n\n\nJudge today's variants. You are the critic: you do NOT edit code. Read\n`journal/2026-10-20/20-brief.md` (the rubric), `30-build.md`, and every\n`journal/2026-10-20/variants/<x>/report.json`. Look at the stills in\n`variants/<x>/stills/` if you can view images. If you cannot, judge from\n`report.json` (exit codes, degraded flags, probe dims), the variant's\n`src/<slug>.ts` and `src/<slug>.layout.m0` (the flattened layout — read the\nrectangle counts and proportions), and `node tools/check-registry.mjs --json`.\n\nBe adversarial. The default is \"no ship\". A template earns a ship.\n\nRead every still the way a stranger would, string by string, before you score\nanything. A number that is malformed (`2:030.123` for a lap time), a sign\nthat is missing, a unit nothing explains, defaults that contradict what the\nbrief's \"Defaults must show\" promised: each is a defect on line 2 or line 8,\nand none of them is in `report.json`. Day 013's first critique found the\nmissing bars and passed the malformed times beside them.\n\nA no-ship is not the end of the day: the build phase is called again with\nyour verdict in hand, and you (a fresh call) judge the result. So \"what each\nvariant gets wrong\" is the builder's worklist — name every defect you found,\nspecifically, not only the worst one.\n\n## Score each variant, 0–2 on each line\n\n1. Renders at defaults, every canvas, no error mosaic (exit 0 everywhere; a 3 anywhere is fatal)\n2. The defaults SHOW the use case — a stranger sees what it is for\n3. Text fits and reads at portrait and square, not only landscape\n4. Layout is honest rectangles: proportions hold across aspects, nothing overlaps or collapses; the\n   layout contract promises what the brief said (read `layoutContract()` in `src/<slug>.ts`: text fits,\n   the bands the design depends on - not a ruler widened until it passes)\n5. Props are the few that matter, typed, with sane defaults; a user could actually feed it\n6. Would a person in the scouted community use this instead of what they do now?\n7. Code quality against AGENTS.md: deterministic, `ctx.target`, bound text, fitted copy, ASCII\n8. The brief was honest — the variant does what `20-brief.md` promised\n9. The why-tutorial tells the truth — `stills/tutorial-2.png` (the problem)\n   cites what `10-scout.md` found, in the evidence's words, with its sources;\n   `stills/tutorial-3.png` (the solution) describes what THIS variant does,\n   not what the brief hoped; `tutorial-5.png` is the template at its defaults;\n   `tutorial-6.png` (how it was made) shows the phases that actually ran\n\nFatal regardless of score: a degraded render, a test that only passes because\nit asserts nothing, a prop with no default, a pack or slug that misrepresents\nwhat it is, a why-tutorial whose problem page names sources the scout never\nopened or whose solution page claims a feature the variant does not have.\n\n## Output — `journal/2026-10-20/40-critique.md`\n\n```\n# Critique — 2026-10-20\n\n## Scores\n| variant | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | total | fatal? |\n## What each variant gets right / wrong (three lines each, specific: which still, which prop)\n## Decision: SHIP <letter> | NO SHIP\n## If ship: what a human polish pass should look at first\n## If no ship: the one thing that would have changed the verdict\n```\n\nThen set `state.json.decision` to `\"ship\"` or `\"no-ship\"`, `state.json.pick` to\nthe letter (or null), `noShipReason` when no-ship (one line), and finally\n`critique: \"done\"`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":135,"totalLines":135,"truncated":false}},"title":"journal\\2026-10-20\\logs\\critique.prompt.md","time":{"start":1792530951625,"end":1792530951656}},"id":"prt_15b2bacf7001PfNueiIQu7mKLz","sessionID":"ses_ea4d52decffeaHsc6A3RWeHtGB","messageID":"msg_15b2b98bd001Hc3UCH6Cx3ka4j"}}
